IOCTL(2)                       P32W Unix                        IOCTL(2)

NAME
       ioctl, gtty, stty - control a terminal

SYNOPSIS
       #include <sgtty.h>

       int ioctl(int fd, int cmd, void *arg);
       int gtty(int fd, struct sgttyb *b);
       int stty(int fd, struct sgttyb *b);

              struct sgttyb {
                  char sg_ispeed, sg_ospeed;  /* unused */
                  char sg_erase;              /* erase character */
                  char sg_kill;               /* line kill character */
                  int sg_flags;               /* modes */
              };

DESCRIPTION
       ioctl performs the device-specific operation cmd on the
       open device fd. Only the terminals, /dev/tty0, /dev/tty1 and
       /dev/tty, have any; they take Unix V7's two:

       TIOCGETP (1)
              Store the terminal's modes in the sgttyb at arg.
       TIOCSETP (2)
              Set the modes from the sgttyb at arg. It takes effect
              at once, for characters already typed too.

       gtty(fd, b) is ioctl(fd, TIOCGETP, b) and stty(fd, b) is
       ioctl(fd, TIOCSETP, b). The library's isatty(fd) is true
       when gtty succeeds.

       sg_flags is an or of

       ECHO   (010)   echo what is typed
       CRMOD  (020)   map a typed carriage return to newline, and
                      send a newline as carriage return, newline
       CBREAK (02)    give characters to read(2) as they come,
                      without erase, kill or ^D processing; ^C
                      and ^\ still send signals
       RAW    (040)   give every character, all 8 bits, as it
                      comes, with no processing at all

       Every terminal starts with ECHO | CRMOD, erase ^H and kill
       ^U, at 9600 baud. TIOCSETP replaces all of these at once, so
       read the modes with gtty, change them, and write them back:

              struct sgttyb b;
              gtty(0, &b);
              b.sg_flags |= CBREAK;
              b.sg_flags &= ~ECHO;
              stty(0, &b);

       THE NORMAL MODE
       Without CBREAK or RAW, input is collected a line at a time:
       read(2) waits until a line is finished and returns at most
       one line. While a line is typed:

       erase (sg_erase) or DEL
              removes the last character (echoed as backspace,
              space, backspace).
       kill (sg_kill)
              throws the line away (echoed, then a newline).
       ^D     ends the line without a newline; at the start of a
              line, read returns 0 (end of file).
       ^C, ^\ throw away everything typed and not yet read, echo,
              and send SIGINT or SIGQUIT to every process whose
              controlling terminal this is. This holds in CBREAK
              mode too.

       Input is 7 bits (the top bit is stripped) except in RAW
       mode. Other control characters echo as ^X. A line holds at
       most 4095 characters; more are dropped.

RETURN VALUE
       0 on success; -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       EBADF   fd is not an open file descriptor.
       ENOTTY  fd is not a character device, or its device has no
               ioctl operations (/dev/null, /dev/port, /dev/rdisk).
       EINVAL  cmd is not TIOCGETP or TIOCSETP.
       ENXIO   fd is /dev/tty and the process has no controlling
               terminal.
       EFAULT  arg is a bad address.

SEE ALSO
       stty(1), open(2), read(2), signal(2), getty(8)

BUGS
       The speed cannot be changed: sg_ispeed and sg_ospeed read as
       0 and are ignored. There is no TIOCSETN, TIOCFLUSH, TIOCEXCL
       or TIOCHPCL, no tchars or ltchars: the interrupt, quit and
       end-of-file characters are fixed at ^C, ^\ and ^D; there is
       no ^S/^Q flow control, no tab expansion, no LCASE and no
       parity. RAW mode does not echo, even with ECHO set, and does
       not turn off CRMOD's newline mapping on output. TIOCSETP
       neither waits for output to drain nor flushes input, unlike
       V7's.
